    "q-105156": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105156",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find $t$, if $2{a}_{1}+t{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $t$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+t{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n-4t=-16\\,⟹\\,t=4.\n$$\n\nBut then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $t$ such that \n $2{a}_{1}+t{a}_{2}=0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
